"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000185721","module":"turquoise","kme":0.0881268,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.796936,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000185721","module":"turquoise","kme":0.205761,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.524491,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000185721","module":"turquoise","kme":0.10693,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.680759,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000185721","module":"turquoise","kme":0.0456101,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.842971,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000185721","entrez_id":["4733"],"uniprot":["Q9Y295"],"ncbi_summary":"Enables several functions, including GTPase activity; identical protein binding activity; and potassium ion binding activity. Involved in positive regulation of microtubule polymerization and regulation of mitotic spindle assembly. Located in cytosol and nuclear body. Part of polysome. [provided by Alliance of Genome Resources, Apr 2022]","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q9Y295","text":"Catalyzes the conversion of GTP to GDP through hydrolysis of the gamma-phosphate bond in GTP (PubMed:23711155, PubMed:29915238, PubMed:37179472). Appears to have an intrinsic GTPase activity that is stimulated by ZC3H15/DFRP1 binding likely by increasing the affinity for the potassium ions (PubMed:23711155). When hydroxylated at C-3 of 'Lys-22' by JMJD7, may bind to RNA and play a role in translation (PubMed:19819225, PubMed:29915238). Binds to microtubules and promotes microtubule polymerization and stability that are required for mitotic spindle assembly during prophase to anaphase transition. GTPase activity is not necessary for these microtubule-related functions (PubMed:28855639)."}]